    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d246">

                <pb facs="0251" n="239" xml:id="pg239"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">845.00/7–2544: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-25T00:00:00-04:00">July 25,
                            1944—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5828. For the Ambassador and Phillips. Drew Pearson’s column today prints large
                    part of Phillips letter to President<anchor n="26" xml:id="d246-f1"/> on Indian
                    situation in which he advocated that British King Emperor issue declaration with
                    respect to later independence for India after the war.</p>
                <p>This publication was made without any prior knowledge of the Department and we do
                    not know how Pearson came into possession of copy of this letter addressed to
                    the President.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
